Lucent @VERSION@: third-party notices
====================================

Lucent is copyright (c) 2026 Fan Zhu and distributed under the MIT License (Lucent-LICENSE.txt in
this folder), except its logo (below). It includes the third-party components listed below, each
under its own licence. This list is generated from the files that this installation actually
contains (tools/package.ps1), so it names exactly the components that ship.

@LUCENT_LOGO@

- "DLL" components are separate files in the installation folder. "Compiled in" components are
  header-only libraries, and parts of the compiler's own libraries, whose code is part of
  @PROGRAMS@; code "linked statically" into a program is named
  with that program and its own licence.
- A component's parts under licences of their own are listed with it, each with the file of its
  notice and terms in the folder named after the component. Each file says where its texts come
  from: the component's source archive (for the compiler's library, its header in the compiler
  that built Lucent; for Inno Setup, its published source), or, where that archive names a
  licence without its text, another component's licence file in this folder that holds it.
- The full copyright and licence text of each vcpkg component is in the folder named after it, in
  copyright.txt (copied from the component's vcpkg package) and any further files listed. The
  compiler's library (msvc-stl) and the Windows SDK's code inside Qt's files (C++/WinRT) are no
  vcpkg components: msvc-stl's folder holds the notices of its parts (its note says why its
  compiled code needs no licence text), cppwinrt's the licence of C++/WinRT.
- Qt compiles code from other projects into its own files (Unicode data, hash functions, the Public
  Suffix List and more). Each Qt component lists that code with its copyright holders, licence and
  source, as recorded by Qt's own software bill of materials; where that record falls short, the
  list says so (a licence the code carries instead, the lines of its NOTICE, where the Source Code
  Form of copyleft code is). Qt Base also lists the code of the Windows SDK compiled into its files.
- LGPL-NOTICE.txt explains the LGPL libraries (Qt, LibRaw, Lensfun and the GLib libraries it uses):
  your right to replace them, and their corresponding source, the archive
  Lucent-@VERSION@-corresponding-source.zip published next to Lucent's installer.
- The data Lucent ships is listed under "Data" with its licence: Lucent's own data under Lucent's
  MIT License, and data under the terms of its authors (the Lensfun lens database, used unmodified
  under CC BY-SA 3.0) with its licence text in the folder named after it.
- Lucent.spdx.json is the software bill of materials (SPDX 2.3) of this installation.
- The Microsoft Visual C++ Runtime is not part of this folder. If the computer needed it, Lucent
  Setup ran Microsoft's own redistributable installer, unmodified and without its window:
  @VC_REDIST@ (vc_redist.x64.exe). Microsoft's licence terms for it are the
  @VC_REDIST_TERMS@, which that installer shows when it is run on its own.
- Lucent Setup and its uninstaller (unins000.exe) are made with @INNO_SETUP@, copyright
  Jordan Russell and Martijn Laan (https://www.innosetup.com), used unmodified under the Inno
  Setup License for the non-commercial distribution of free software.
@INNO_SETUP_LICENCE@
